{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 220,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energy too cheap to meter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2400,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That was the promise of nuclear power in the 1950s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5600,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least according to Luis Strauss\nchairman of the Atomic Energy Commission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9300,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That promise has not yet come to pass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12660,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but with some incredible new technologies,\nperhaps it still could."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17520,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question is \"should it?\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25440,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Energy isn't scarce. It's everywhere.\nSeriously, literally all mass is energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31420,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The trick is getting at it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33500,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Burn coal and you liberate a tiny bit of the energy\nlocked in its chemical bonds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37360,
    "dDurationMs": 4614,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's easy and cheap to do but the energy you get is pathetic per kilogram of coal"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41980,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and worse per ton of carbon dioxide\nreleased into the atmosphere"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45760,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the other end of the spectrum is the energy released when particles of matter and antimatter are brought together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51039,
    "dDurationMs": 3530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They annihilate each other releasing\na hundred percent of the energy contained"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55180,
    "dDurationMs": 4956,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sounds great except that antimatter\nis incredibly difficult to create and store"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60460,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in between breaking chemical bonds and Matter-antimatter annihilation we have nuclear energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66300,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong nuclear force holding nuclei together contains an enormous amount of energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70930,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Sun is powered that way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72820,
    "dDurationMs": 6439,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Releasing a mere 0.4 percent of the mass of hydrogen nuclei as it fuses them into helium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79299,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that's enough to power the Sun for 10 billion years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83560,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Practical fusion power stations are a holy grail of energy production, but are still a long way off"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90580,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Until then our only viable source\nof nuclear energy is fission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 95880,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means breaking very heavy nuclei\ninto more stable small parts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101320,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we want to convert mass into energy,\nfission gives us the most bang for our buck"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 106500,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, that bang can be literal. Use of nuclear energy may risk the proliferation of nuclear weaponry"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114280,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and there's also the problem of nuclear waste\nand the specter of horrible accidents"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120260,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This last one was painted in terrifying detail\nin the recent dramatization of the Chernobyl disaster"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127300,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclear reactors sounds scary\nbecause the disasters are pretty epic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131920,
    "dDurationMs": 7790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the reality is that far far more people die from straight up air pollution due to coal-fired power plants"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139710,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than ever died in nuclear reactor accidents"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142780,
    "dDurationMs": 7900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, the radioactivity around coal fire plants is also higher due to the traced but completely uncontained"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150680,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radioactive products of coal burning"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152620,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the most compelling attraction is that nuclear power doesn't directly produce carbon emissions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158660,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact nuclear power may be our most sure path to reducing carbon emissions and halting climate change"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165700,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But can we do nuclear power safely enough?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169700,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are modern ideas including the much hyped Thorium Reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174820,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that suggest maybe we can"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177300,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we can understand those,\nwe'll need to review how nuclear reactors work"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182800,
    "dDurationMs": 2869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every fission reactor exploits the same phenomenon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186250,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Certain very large nuclei like uranium and plutonium\ncan split into smaller nuclei when hit by a single neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193690,
    "dDurationMs": 3979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When these nuclei split,\nthey release energy and fast-moving neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197890,
    "dDurationMs": 5900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those new neutrons can smash into nearby nuclei breaking them up and releasing more neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you have enough of these heavy nuclei,\nif you exceed what we call critical mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209880,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then neutrons produced in every fission\ntrigger at least one more fission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214620,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a chain reaction, a domino effect"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That can be a runaway chain reaction in which each split nucleus causes more or other nuclei to split"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Resulting in an explosive release of energy.\nThat would be an atomic bomb"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229080,
    "dDurationMs": 2078,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you can regulate the process,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231158,
    "dDurationMs": 5022,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make sure that each nucleus splitting causes\non average only one other nucleus to break"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236680,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then the reaction can be controlled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239140,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It can be made to produce a steady amount of heat that is used to power a turbine often just by boiling water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246060,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most common commercial power plants use uranium fuel, in particular the isotope"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252080,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Uranium-235"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254680,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Uranium-235 has 92 protons and 143 neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258000,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it makes up less than 1 percent\nof naturally occurring uranium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262460,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which is mostly uranium 238 with three extra neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266980,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "U-235 is useful because it's highly fissile"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270220,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means it has a high probability\nof intercepting a stray Neutron and splitting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274860,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's fissile in the presence of the fast-moving neutrons created by its own fission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279360,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it's many times more fissile if those neutrons are first slowed to become so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286540,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thermal neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand the more stable Uranium 238 is only fissile to fast-moving neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294880,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not at all to slow neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297340,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, is much more likely to absorb slow-moving neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302160,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cheapest way to do commercial fission is to take advantage of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uranium-235's high fissibility to these thermal neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312120,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To sustain thermal fission in uranium you need to enrich it by a few percent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318420,
    "dDurationMs": 3339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "increase the proportion of U-235"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321759,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relative to U-238 so that more neutrons get created and fewer get absorbed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327780,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You also have to slow down those neutrons into the sweet spot for splitting U-235"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333260,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To do this, thermal reactors use some sort of moderator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337900,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most common moderator is plain old water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340810,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because hydrogen nuclei in H2O are around the same mass as neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345759,
    "dDurationMs": 3559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they absorb a lot of momentum in Neutron collisions and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350110,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Conveniently that same water can also work as a coolant. It takes heat away from the uranium fuel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356589,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Preventing melt down to where it's needed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359680,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is to drive a turbine either directly or via a secondary loop of water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364650,
    "dDurationMs": 6689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I just described very very crudely. The principles behind the light water thermal reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371529,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the most common because they're cheapest but let's talk about the problems. First, the safety"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378879,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every major disaster has been with a thermal reactor due to a cooling failure in Three Mile Island"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385089,
    "dDurationMs": 7370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the water escaped a jammed hatch in Chernobyl water boiled increasing the neutron count and in Fukushima a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392919,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tsunami knocked out the water pumps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395800,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The common issue is that water cooling requires active effort to maintain and so is prone to disruption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404050,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Modern light water thermal reactors addressed the failures of the past and repeats of these disasters are very unlikely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410349,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But unforeseen failures are still possible especially due to human error"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415449,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even the smartest nuclear engineer can have a perma Simpson moment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420190,
    "dDurationMs": 4639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way around the coolant issues is to use molten metals or molten salts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425050,
    "dDurationMs": 6500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these can be liquid over a very large range of temperatures reducing the chance of accidental boiling and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431770,
    "dDurationMs": 7339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They allow the system to be operated at much higher temperatures which increases efficiency and at much lower pressure than water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439509,
    "dDurationMs": 6770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the high pressures required for water-cooled reactors add a lot of complexity and size and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446889,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "potential to explode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449379,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the worst downside of the common modern reactor is the waste"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455349,
    "dDurationMs": 5150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They use only around 1% of the uranium extracted from the ground the U-235"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of the U-238 gets converted to fissile plutonium by absorbing neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467320,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But most of it is either unused or converted to heavier non fissile elements. These are the so-called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475260,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Transuranic Actinides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478000,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Elements heavier than uranium on the actinide sequence of the periodic table"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482380,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are very radioactive and have half-lives of tens of thousands of years"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487870,
    "dDurationMs": 6559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means they're dangerous on geological timescales, and there is literally no place on earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494430,
    "dDurationMs": 7470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can guarantee that containment vessels will be safe against earthquakes, volcanic activity or eventual crushing by Ice Age glaciers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503349,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A possible solution to this nightmare waste disposal issue is to try to burn all of the heavy nuclei as fuel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511210,
    "dDurationMs": 7849,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way to do this is to use fast neutrons a fast reactor doesn't try to slow down the neutrons that means"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519940,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "U-238 can split along with U-235 and along with any"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 525400,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actinide that happens to be produced by Neutron absorption"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529090,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The waste products of a fast reactor are the fission products much smaller nuclei than the actinides"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535900,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "some of these are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 537490,
    "dDurationMs": 7369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Incredibly nasty like cesium 137, but they have half-lives of centuries not tens of millennia"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544930,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So safe storage is at least plausible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548800,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast-neutron reactors get to be smaller than their slow thermal cousins because they don't need a neutron moderator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555610,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That makes them ideal for things like submarines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559120,
    "dDurationMs": 4010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The issue with these guys is that you need much more enriched fuel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563530,
    "dDurationMs": 3709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The U-235 content needs to be over 20%"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567880,
    "dDurationMs": 2580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "several times higher than in a thermal reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571080,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that's just because the overall fission rate is much lower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575420,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per fast neutron compared to slow neutrons that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579250,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Enrichment is expensive and so after abundant natural uranium deposits were discovered and fuel became cheap"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586360,
    "dDurationMs": 6950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "commercial interests opted for the thermal reactor even though it wastes 99% of the fuel and leads to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593650,
    "dDurationMs": 3049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "eons of looming environmental catastrophe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597730,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast reactors also have the advantage that they can create or breed their own fuel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604780,
    "dDurationMs": 2839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although fast neutrons don't keep nuclei as easily"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608110,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When they do hit they liberate more free neutrons than when a slow Neutron causes fission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614890,
    "dDurationMs": 2270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Typically 2 to 3 neutrons per split"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618010,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means you have one Neutron to contribute to the fission chain reaction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622120,
    "dDurationMs": 7429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and at least one more to be absorbed by a non fissile element to turn it into something fissile"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630140,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Element that can do this is called fertile"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633760,
    "dDurationMs": 9140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example uranium-238 is fertile because it can absorb a neutron and be transformed into plutonium-239"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643500,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A typical breeder reactor includes a reactor core burning highly enriched uranium or plutonium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650680,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surrounded by a blanket of fertile material that cycles into the core as it becomes fissile"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658420,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thermal and fast reactors have different advantages and disadvantages"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663310,
    "dDurationMs": 5449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Regarding nuclear proliferation the waste of a thermal reactor isn't fissile"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668950,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it could be bred into fissile material"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672040,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ultimate waste products of a fast breeder reactor are not dangerous in this way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678010,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the intermediate products include weapons-grade plutonium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682300,
    "dDurationMs": 7309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which you definitely don't want in the wrong hands some of the advantages of both of these reactor types can be achieved by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690040,
    "dDurationMs": 2119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "switching to a completely different fuel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693220,
    "dDurationMs": 1020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thorium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694240,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's the thorium reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696640,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thorium is another actinide two spaces lighter on the periodic table compared to uranium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701560,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not naturally fissile"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703540,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it is fertile upon absorption of a neutron it decays into protactinium-233"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710200,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and then into uranium-233"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713060,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And U-233 is nicely fissile. It's even better than U-235 and plutonium-239"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719980,
    "dDurationMs": 2359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because it absorbs fewer neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722500,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which means better neutron economy and more importantly on average uranium-233"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Produces slightly more than two neutrons per split even when it's split by a slow-moving neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735760,
    "dDurationMs": 5869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it's possible to breed new uranium-233 from thorium in a thermal reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742000,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You don't need a fast reactor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744220,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are different ways to build a thorium reactor, but perhaps most promising is the liquid fluoride thorium reactor or LFTR"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752200,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this design both thorium and uranium-233 are bonded with fluorine and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759160,
    "dDurationMs": 4039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dissolved in a molten fluoride salt beryllium or lithium fluoride"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763870,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the fission in the uranium produces heat and neutrons to sustain fission and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769030,
    "dDurationMs": 2789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to breed more uranium from the thorium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772340,
    "dDurationMs": 7339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The uranium and thorium can either be mixed together or separated with a thorium blanket surrounding the uranium core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780200,
    "dDurationMs": 3770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In either case the molten salt containing the uranium"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784310,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also transports heat out of the core to secondary circuits that ultimately power turbine"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 790250,
    "dDurationMs": 3049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The actual fusion only happens in the reactor core"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793850,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because that's where the moderator"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 796280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Slows down the neutrons to make fission much more likely in this case"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 801160,
    "dDurationMs": 4139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The moderator is a lattice of graphite channels through which the fluid flows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806000,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Graphite is particularly great because it slows neutrons without absorbing them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811280,
    "dDurationMs": 5989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When the fluid is away from the graphite neutrons speed up which means fission slows down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818270,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because it's in liquid form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820190,
    "dDurationMs": 5989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fuel can be quickly drained from the reactor in emergencies a plug with a low melting temperature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826580,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Will melt if the core gets too hot or if power supplying a cooling fan goes out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 833240,
    "dDurationMs": 3619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The fuel then drains to a tank where fission is impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837170,
    "dDurationMs": 5719,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in addition built the right way, the liquid fuel becomes less fissile as temperature increases"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 843680,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's because at high temperatures thorium is increasingly good at absorbing neutrons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848870,
    "dDurationMs": 2929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So not enough neutrons are left to continue to fission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852500,
    "dDurationMs": 2540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this whole setup is a great example of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855560,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Passive or walkaway safety meaning that in the event of an emergency"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860180,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Even if every mechanical or human mechanism failed the reactor would simply power down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866990,
    "dDurationMs": 6619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another compelling advantage of the lifter and molten salt reactors in general is that they can be small because they don't need giant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873680,
    "dDurationMs": 2119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structures to handle the high pressure water"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876050,
    "dDurationMs": 770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in fact"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876820,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was for use in submarines and aircraft that molten coolant reactors were first conceived"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882590,
    "dDurationMs": 2330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now this compactness and modularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 885470,
    "dDurationMs": 7639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "means they could be inserted into the current electrical grid to replace coal or natural gas plants or you know on a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893360,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Lunar or Martian settlement or a starship that same modularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898220,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Poses perhaps the biggest risk if small thorium reactors became widespread"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903740,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They'd become less easy to regulate and monitor"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906890,
    "dDurationMs": 6619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we'd want to be very careful that the reactor design leaves the weaponizable U-233"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 914360,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "completely inaccessible without"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 916490,
    "dDurationMs": 1349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enormous effort"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917839,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nuclear power is a possible solution to our dire energy and climate challenges"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 922490,
    "dDurationMs": 6590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question is do we need it or can we meet those challenges with renewables like wind and solar?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929839,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assuming significant advances in battery tech"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933560,
    "dDurationMs": 2929,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don't know the answer and I'd love to hear  your opinions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936980,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What I do know is that we face an enormous hurdle in our progression as a technological species one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 943690,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which may take all of the ingenuity we can master"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946790,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We should think very carefully about whether the power of the atom is necessary to survive and thrive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954230,
    "dDurationMs": 7250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Into the next technological stage and send us to greater distances and further futures in space-time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962020,
    "dDurationMs": 6959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a recent episode we talked about how black holes influence the galaxies they formed in often by killing them"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969140,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's see what you had to say"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 971270,
    "dDurationMs": 5539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Steve C comments that this whole black hole killing star formation thing seems like a negative feedback"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 977150,
    "dDurationMs": 7399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interaction more gas equals more active black hole equals more outward radiation and wind equals less gas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984980,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suitable for star formation or feeding a black hole like a thermostat at the center of a galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991160,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well put, Steve! That's exactly what it is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994820,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chuck Ritter's Dorf asks, whether quasar jets and a preferred direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999710,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Relative to the galaxy as a whole and how does this influence their effect on the galaxy?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005560,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so full blown quasars the most luminous of accreting black holes or active galactic nuclei are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012160,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Typically in bowl-like elliptical galaxies. So orientation isn't as meaningful as in a spiral galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1019620,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "weaker active galactic nuclei tend to live in spiral galaxies for example Seyfert galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025620,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they can have their Jets pointing in any direction at right angles to the disk or even straight into the disk"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032320,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in general when such a jet is first launched"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035420,
    "dDurationMs": 4609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It tends to balloon out and spread its energy through a good fraction of the galaxy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040029,
    "dDurationMs": 7971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So orientation isn't so important. Proghead777 asks, whether the central supermassive black holes gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048000,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Influence is extended by frame dragging"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052070,
    "dDurationMs": 3109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer is yes, but not very far"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1055700,
    "dDurationMs": 6169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Frame dragging is the dragging of the fabric of space around a rotating massive object in the case of a black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1062080,
    "dDurationMs": 7220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's most obvious effect is that it changes how closely an objects can orbit the black hole in a stable way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069760,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If objects orbit in the same direction as a black hole's rotation that can be stable much closer in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075410,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but if they orbit in the opposite direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077020,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then the stability limit is further out this results in a black hole shadow a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1082550,
    "dDurationMs": 4579,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Blank region in the middle of the accretion disk that depends on black hole spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087230,
    "dDurationMs": 2750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's one way we'll be able to measure that spin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090200,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But this definitely doesn't extend more than 10 or so times the black hole event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095600,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Oppie asks why NASA isn't dedicating more resources to investigating all West themed planets in elliptical galaxies"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1103200,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I don't know, Oppie. I just don't know."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106280,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Probably the same evil conspiracy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1108700,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That got Firefly cancelled after one season"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1111920,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why do the powers that be fear space cowboys so much?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
